---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Accessories"
    categorySlug="gear"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Three Months, Eight Keyboards, One Very Patient Partner</h2>
  <p>
    Eight keyboards in three months. My partner stopped asking questions around keyboard number four.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Dell membrane board from 2019 gave me 43 WPM on typing tests. That number bugged me. Also my wrists hurt constantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Switched to a $60 mechanical. Sixty-eight words per minute now. No more cramping. The specs matter
    here—50 million keystroke rating versus 5 million on membrane. Cherry MX Browns at 45g actuation versus
    that mushy 65g membrane resistance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three years into this hobby. Tested maybe a dozen keyboards for this article. My partner has opinions about the
    clicking. Strong opinions. But here we are.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> Get a <a href="#keychron-k2">Keychron K2</a>. Eighty-nine dollars, works with
    Mac and Windows out of the box, wireless that doesn't suck. That's the one next to me right now as I type this. If
    your job involves a lot of video calls, the <a href="#logitech-mx">Logitech MX Mechanical</a> has these tactile
    quiet switches that are actually... quiet. Novel concept.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks If You're In a Rush</h2>
  <p>My partner is staring at me because dinner's ready so let me speed through this:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 mechanical keyboards for remote work"
  />
  <p>
    Want the nerdy details on each? Keep scrolling. Or skip to the switch type section because that matters more than
    most people realize.
  </p>

  <!-- Switch Types -->
  <h2 id="switch-types">Understanding Those Clicky Bits Under the Keys</h2>
  <p>
    Mechanical keyboards have these things called switches. Three main varieties:
  </p>

  <h3>Linear (the smooth ones)</h3>
  <p>
    You press down and it goes down. No bump, no click, just a smooth journey to the bottom. Cherry MX Reds are what
    gamers use. I personally find them boring but some people swear by them for fast typing.
  </p>

  <h3>Tactile (the bumpy ones)</h3>
  <p>
    There's this subtle bump halfway through the press that tells your fingers "hey, that keystroke registered." Cherry
    MX Browns are the classic choice. This is what most typists end up liking, myself included.
  </p>

  <h3>Clicky (the loud ones)</h3>
  <p>
    That typewriter sound your grandparents made. Cherry MX Blues. I genuinely love these. My partner does not. Use them
    if you work alone or actively enjoy annoying people.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>My advice for remote workers?</strong> Tactile switches. You get the satisfying feedback without making your
    Zoom colleagues want to strangle you.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Reviews -->
  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">The Part Where I Review Keyboards I Actually Own</h2>

  <!-- Keychron K2 -->
  <h3 id="keychron-k2">Keychron K2 — My Daily Driver</h3>
  <p>
    If everything I own caught fire tomorrow and I had to replace one keyboard, I'd buy this again without hesitation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does it work so well? Hard to say exactly. It's just... competent at everything. Mac support is actually good,
    not an afterthought. Windows support is good. Bluetooth connects to three things and switching between them takes
    maybe two seconds. The 75% layout gives you function keys and arrows while ditching that numpad nobody uses.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mine's been going strong for around eight months. I dropped it once—long story involving a cat—and the aluminum
    frame survived fine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Things that bug me:</strong> the keyboard sits pretty high so you probably want a wrist rest. Stock keycaps
    feel plasticky; I replaced mine eventually. And every week or so the Bluetooth has this tiny hiccup where it
    hesitates for half a second. Not a dealbreaker but I notice it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Somewhere between <strong>$89 and $109</strong> depending on which variant you pick. I went with Brown switches
    because I have no imagination.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/keychron-k2"
      text="Check Keychron K2 Price"
      variant="primary"
      program="keychron"
  />

  <!-- Das Keyboard 4 Professional -->
  <h3 id="das-keyboard">Das Keyboard 4 Professional — Built Like a German Tank</h3>
  <p>
    This thing will outlive cockroaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    Das Keyboard has been making mechanical keyboards since back when that was weird instead of trendy. The Model 4 is
    their flagship. Full aluminum construction, genuine Cherry MX switches, and this hilariously oversized volume knob
    that I didn't think I wanted until I used one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The problems:</strong> $169 is genuinely expensive. No wireless option whatsoever. Full-size layout means it
    devours desk space. And no Mac-specific keycaps if that matters to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    But if you want something that feels like it was forged in actual fire? My friend still uses his from 2011. Not even
    joking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $169</strong>
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/das-keyboard-4"
      text="Check Das Keyboard 4 Price on Amazon"
      variant="secondary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Logitech MX Mechanical -->
  <h3 id="logitech-mx">Logitech MX Mechanical — For Serial Device Switchers</h3>
  <p>
    Some days I'm on my MacBook. Then I'm on my Windows desktop. Then back to the MacBook. Then I'm remoting into a work
    machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    This keyboard has a button that swaps between three paired devices instantly. Press it, boom, now you're typing on
    the other computer. Logitech's Easy-Switch thing actually works and I use it constantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Their Tactile Quiet switches are also shockingly quiet. I've been on video calls typing on this thing and nobody
    complained. Usually someone complains.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Downsides:</strong> $149 to $169 range. Can't swap switches if you decide you hate them. And Logitech uses
    their own switches instead of Cherry MX, which apparently bothers some keyboard enthusiasts. Doesn't bother me
    personally.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $149 (Mini), $169 (Full)</strong>
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/logitech-mx-mechanical"
      text="Check Logitech MX Mechanical Price"
      variant="secondary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Keychron Q1 -->
  <h3 id="keychron-q1">Keychron Q1 — For People Who Watch Keyboard Sound Test Videos</h3>
  <p>
    You know those YouTube videos where someone carefully applies lubricant to each individual switch and then taps keys
    for ten minutes while whispering about "thock"? If you watch those, this keyboard is for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Q1 is the K2's more expensive sibling. Full aluminum case. Gasket mount which makes it sound... thocky? Is that
    even a word? It is now. QMK support means you can program literally every key to do whatever you want.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> $179. Wired only, no Bluetooth. Weighs about 1.7 kilograms which means you could
    theoretically defend yourself with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The typing feel is noticeably better than budget boards though. If you care about this stuff, you'll care about this
    keyboard.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $179</strong>
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/keychron-q1"
      text="Check Keychron Q1 Price"
      variant="secondary"
      program="keychron"
  />

  <!-- NuPhy Air75 -->
  <h3 id="nuphy-air75">NuPhy Air75 — The Flat One</h3>
  <p>
    Standard mechanical keyboards are chunky. The NuPhy Air75 exists for people who look at that chunk and say
    "absolutely not."
  </p>
  <p>
    Low-profile switches. Sits almost flat on your desk. I took mine traveling for a month and it fit in my laptop
    sleeve no problem. Doesn't need a wrist rest because the whole thing is only 16mm tall.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the weird part about low-profile switches though: people either love them or hate them. Not much middle
    ground there. If you're coming from typing on a laptop keyboard and want something similar but upgraded, give this a
    shot.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $109.</strong> Mac keycaps included which is thoughtful.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/nuphy-air75"
      text="Check NuPhy Air75 Price on Amazon"
      variant="ghost"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Ducky One 3 -->
  <h3 id="ducky-one">Ducky One 3 — Good Luck Finding One</h3>
  <p>
    Ducky keyboards have genuinely excellent keycaps. The PBT double-shot ones on the One 3 won't get that gross shiny
    look after a year of use like cheap ABS caps do.
  </p>
  <p>
    The actual problem: they're perpetually out of stock. Every time I look, sold out. If you find one available, grab
    it before someone else does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $129 to $149</strong> depending on the size. Wired only. Works fine with Mac even without
    Mac-specific legends.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/ducky-one-3"
      text="Check Ducky One 3 Price on Amazon"
      variant="ghost"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Leopold FC660M -->
  <h3 id="leopold">Leopold FC660M — The Purist's Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    No RGB lighting. No wireless. No hot-swap sockets. No gimmicks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just really, really good typing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Leopold is what keyboard nerds recommend when asked what keyboard nerds actually use. The FC660M has sound dampening
    inside that makes it quieter than most Cherry MX boards. Keycaps are excellent. Build quality is excellent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $119</strong> and it does exactly one thing extremely well. That's the entire sales pitch.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/leopold-fc660m"
      text="Check Leopold FC660M Price on Amazon"
      variant="ghost"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Royal Kludge RK84 -->
  <h3 id="royal-kludge">Royal Kludge RK84 — This Price Has to Be a Typo</h3>
  <p>
    Sixty dollars. For a mechanical keyboard with hot-swap switches, Bluetooth, 2.4GHz wireless dongle, wired mode, AND
    RGB lighting.
  </p>
  <p>
    There has to be a catch, right? Kind of. Stock switches are mediocre. Keycaps are cheap ABS. The software works but
    barely.
  </p>
  <p>
    But consider this math: you could buy this keyboard, hate the switches, buy nice replacement switches, install them
    yourself, and still spend less money than one Keychron Q1. At sixty dollars it's basically a platform for
    experimentation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Price: $60</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're new to all this mechanical keyboard stuff and don't know what you like yet? Start here. Figure things out.
    Upgrade later if you want.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/rk84"
      text="Check Royal Kludge RK84 Price on Amazon"
      variant="ghost"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <!-- Quiet Switches -->
  <h2 id="quiet-switches">Video Call Survival Guide</h2>
  <p>
    Keyboard choice matters less than switch choice when you're on calls all day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The quiet options:</strong> Cherry MX Silent Reds are nearly silent. Gateron Silent Browns exist. Logitech
    puts those Tactile Quiet switches in their MX line. Boba U4 switches are popular in the hot-swap community.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Other things that help:</strong> desk mats absorb impact sound, O-ring dampeners are a cheap mod, and
    software like Krisp or RTX Voice can filter out keyboard noise in real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've made clicky switches work on calls before. It requires some effort but it's doable.
  </p>

  <!-- Wireless vs Wired -->
  <h2 id="wireless-vs-wired">The Wireless Question</h2>
  <p>
    Modern wireless keyboards basically have no lag anymore. Maybe competitive gamers can perceive a difference? I can't
    and I've tried.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wireless means cleaner desk aesthetics and easier device switching. Wired means you never charge anything and save
    maybe twenty dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several keyboards do both. The Keychron K2 and RK84 can use Bluetooth or plug in with USB-C. Best of both
    approaches.
  </p>

  <!-- Mac Compatibility -->
  <h2 id="mac-compatibility">Mac Users Need to Pay Attention Here</h2>
  <p>
    Keyboards don't automatically work well with Mac. The key positions are different, Command and Control swap places,
    it becomes a whole situation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keychron builds for Mac first. Mac keycaps in the box, physical switch to change layouts. Logitech handles this
    through software. NuPhy includes Mac legends.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a keyboard doesn't specifically mention Mac compatibility, assume you'll be remapping keys for hours and still
    annoyed after.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">So What Am I Actually Recommending</h2>
  <p>
    Three months of testing. Eight different keyboards lived on my desk at various points. My conclusions:
